#{[17592186116549 #edb/ref 1064 "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1065 7628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d01e4be3-25c3-5922-9f88-c8eb7538e62e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116549 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315059 #edb/ref 1759218